# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is not,
# so never put a real value here.

# ---- which of the two apps this is -----------------------------------------
# Nothing. There is no mode variable and there must not be one: the mode is
# whether Pollar has a session, evaluated in the browser (lib/app-mode.ts).
#
#   preview     nobody signed in   we pay, from the demo wallet below
#   production  a Pollar session   they pay, from their own account
#
# Signing in *is* the crossing. Deriving it this way is what makes the mode
# unable to disagree with who you are — which is why the variables below are
# grouped by which of the two apps needs them, and why a deployment that sets
# only the preview half is a complete, working demo rather than a broken
# install. See DEPLOY.md Part 2.

# ---- the agent -------------------------------------------------------------
# console.anthropic.com -> API keys. Server-side only: it is what /api/chat
# uses to run the shopping agent.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---- sign-in, which is also the mode (production) --------------------------
# pollar.xyz dashboard -> your application -> API key. Publishable: it ships in
# the browser bundle by design, which is why the name starts with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# One key, and it is the mainnet one. A Pollar key is network-scoped and the
# project holds a key for mainnet only, so there is no signed-in preview and
# no `NEXT_PUBLIC_POLLAR_API_KEY` for testnet — that variable is gone, and
# pasting a value into it does nothing. The name keeps its `_MAINNET` suffix
# because the key really is network-scoped; if a second one is ever issued,
# the map in lib/pollar.ts is where it goes.
#
# Leave it empty and preview still works end to end — chat, search, cart, a
# real payment from the demo wallet and a real card. What is missing is the
# way *out* of preview: the sign-in button says so rather than failing later.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POLLAR_API_KEY_MAINNET=

# ---- the backend signer ----------------------------------------------------
# The secret for `changuito-resolver`, the key that mints demo USDC for the
# faucet and settles or refunds escrow orders. Print it with:
#
#     stellar keys show changuito-resolver
#
# Server-side only. It is a testnet key holding play money, but it is still a
# secret key: it never gets a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix and never reaches the client.
STELLAR_RESOLVER_SECRET=

# The resolver for modo real, and it must be a *different key*, generated
# fresh — not the one above. The testnet secret has been through a deploy
# script and a shell history; real money must not depend on that. The app
# checks the derived public key against the resolver recorded for that network
# in deployments.json and refuses at the first call if they disagree, so a
# secret pasted into the wrong slot fails loudly instead of signing on a chain
# nobody meant to touch. Print it with:
#
#     stellar keys show changuito-resolver-mainnet
#
# Server-side only, and unlike the key above this one holds real XLM.
STELLAR_RESOLVER_SECRET_MAINNET=

# ---- the test faucet -------------------------------------------------------
# Who may press "Cargar USDC". The faucet mints with the key above, so it is
# deny-by-default: in production (Vercel Previews included) an empty value
# disables it for everyone. List the Pollar wallet address (G…) of each test
# account, comma separated; it is shown under "Tu pago" when logged in. The
# browser proves the wallet with a SEP-53 signature, which Pollar only makes
# for a logged-in session, so a typed address alone gets a 401.
# Unset locally, the faucet stays open for development.
# FAUCET_ALLOWLIST_ADDRESSES=GABC...,GDEF...

# The way out of the list, for a demo anyone should be able to try: set this
# and the allowlist above is not consulted at all, whatever it contains. It
# does *not* waive the SEP-53 signature — the wallet still signs, which costs a
# logged-in visitor nothing and costs a script the whole exercise. That is
# load-bearing: the grants share one hourly ceiling, so an unauthenticated
# faucet is a way for one bot to spend every tester's twenty. Only the literal
# values 1, true, yes or on turn it on; "false" and "0" leave it off, which is
# what somebody means when they type them into a dashboard with no checkbox.
# FAUCET_OPEN_TO_ALL=1

# ---- paying by deposit, and the card ---------------------------------------
# Where a shopper's deposit is paid into. A G… account you control; the address
# is public by nature (it is printed on screen to be paid) and *its* secret is
# deliberately nowhere near this deployment — nothing here can pay out of a
# deposit account, on either network, and a refund is done by hand. Unset, the
# deposit screen answers 503.
# A C… contract address is refused: it cannot take a classic payment with a
# memo, and the memo is how the payment is recognised.
#
# One open item, recorded here because it is cheap now and expensive later:
# the two are currently the *same* address. Separate ledgers, so not a
# collision — but then one secret key controls both the demo's play money and
# every real deposit. Splitting them costs a keypair.
# DEPOSIT_ADDRESS_TESTNET=GABC...
# DEPOSIT_ADDRESS_MAINNET=GABC...

# ---- the demo wallet (preview) ---------------------------------------------
# The one secret key this deployment holds, and the reason preview can settle
# a real payment for a visitor who has no wallet: `POST /api/deposit/demo`
# signs with it. Testnet only.
#
# Set it to the secret of the account pinned as `demoWallet` in
# deployments.json (testnet GDFF477UQU…MINA55X). The route derives the public
# key and compares; a key that is not that one throws at the first call rather
# than signing. `demoWallet` is `""` on mainnet, so a mainnet secret pasted
# here can never match and the demo route cannot run there at all.
#
# What it can do is bounded by four things, not by intent — see
# lib/server/demo-wallet.ts. Short version: the asset is testnet USDC from an
# issuer whose master weight is zero (a billion exists, no more ever will), the
# destination is DEPOSIT_ADDRESS_TESTNET with no parameter to change it, the
# network is fixed, and the value is read at call time so a build without it
# still succeeds.
#
# Server-side only. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never echoed, not even a prefix.
# Unset, preview quotes and then cannot pay: the button answers 503 and says so.
# DEMO_WALLET_SECRET=

# ---- the card --------------------------------------------------------------
# Vyrion dashboard -> Settings -> API Keys. Unset, the card button is never
# rendered rather than rendered and broken.
# VYRION_API_KEY=sk_test_...

# The four characters between the sandbox and somebody's money. An sk_live_
# key is refused unless this is exactly "1" — a key beginning sk_test_ ignores
# it entirely, so the only way to spend real money is to have typed both.
# ALLOW_LIVE=1

# ---- who may use modo real, and who may pay --------------------------------
# Same format and the same deny-by-default rule as the faucet list above: empty
# in production means nobody, Vercel Previews included. It is enforced *after*
# the wallet's SEP-53 signature is verified, so it is checked against a proven
# address rather than a claimed one — the closed control in the browser is a
# courtesy, and this is the wall. Passkey smart wallets (C…) cannot sign that
# message, so testers need a custodial G… account.
#
# It guards both rails: the escrow (lib/settle-gate.ts) and the deposit + card
# checkout (lib/deposit-gate.ts). Four states, and what each means at checkout:
#
#   open       not production, no list   -> pays with no signature (next dev)
#   allowlist  the list is non-empty     -> on the list, and signs once
#   public     REAL_MODE_OPEN_TO_ALL set -> anybody, and still signs once
#   disabled   production, empty list    -> refused before anything is quoted
#
# Testnet is none of these — it is the default network, it cannot spend
# anything, and modo prueba checkout needs no wallet at all.
# REAL_MODE_ALLOWLIST_ADDRESSES=GABC...,GDEF...

# And the way out of *that* list. Same yes-words as the faucet's switch, and
# deliberately a separate variable, because the two waive very different
# things: one hands out play money, this one lets any wallet open an escrow
# with real USDC. Read lib/settle-gate.ts before setting it — "ya lo completé"
# is still the buyer's word, so anyone who can reach modo real can complete a
# purchase at the super and then refund themselves, and the treasury eats it.
# The allowlist is what makes that hole acceptable; this accepts it for
# everybody. It deploys nothing: with mainnet unconfigured the mode is still
# closed for all, the flag just stops being the reason why.
#
# It drops the list, not the signature. "Anybody may pay" and "nobody has to
# prove who they are" are different sentences, and only the first is on offer:
# a wallet still signs "pagar la compra" before /api/deposit quotes anything.
# REAL_MODE_OPEN_TO_ALL=1

# ---- the human gate --------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare dashboard -> Turnstile -> add a site. The site key is rendered
# into the widget; the secret verifies the token server-side.
#
# The secret alone decides the gate: set it and the gate enforces, leave it
# unset in production and the gate is "closed" — middleware.ts then answers 403
# `solo_humanos` on every /api/* route except /api/human, so chat, balance,
# quote, faucet and settle are all dead. That is deliberate: an open agent
# endpoint on a public URL bills someone's Anthropic key, and failing shut is
# the only safe way to be misconfigured.
#
# The site key deliberately does not affect the mode. It is public, and a
# missing read of it must never close the gate while the secret is set — that
# would 403 every route before Turnstile ever had a chance to run. Set it
# anyway: without it the widget never mounts, so nobody can pass the gate.
#
# Unset both in development and the gate is "open", so localhost works with no
# Cloudflare account.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# Optional alias for the site key, read only if the NEXT_PUBLIC_ one is empty.
# It exists because Next and Vercel only ship a variable the source mentions as
# a literal `process.env.NAME`; a bracket lookup is invisible to that pass, and
# production once reported both keys missing and never mounted the widget. Set
# the NEXT_PUBLIC_ name — this is a fallback, not a second thing to configure.
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# Required in production. Signs the httpOnly `chg_user` cookie that records a
# Pollar login, which lifts the three-free-turn guest limit on /api/chat.
# The cookie is only issued after the wallet signs a login message (SEP-53),
# so it cannot be minted for an address someone merely typed.
#
# Production uses this and nothing else: unset, no one can sign in (the login
# route answers 503) and signed-in shoppers are treated as guests. Locally it
# falls back to TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY, then to a dev constant. Any long random
# string, different from the Turnstile secret:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Rotating it signs every existing user out, which is harmless: the cookie
# carries a wallet address, not a session anyone will miss.
# CHG_SESSION_SECRET=

# ---- the database (production) ---------------------------------------------
# Supabase -> Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string. Two of them,
# and the difference between them is the part that fails only under load, so
# it is worth reading once:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  port 6543, the *transaction* pooler. This is what the app
#                 opens. A lambda handles one request at a time, holds the
#                 connection for that request and gives it back.
#   DIRECT_URL    port 5432, a session connection. Migrations and
#                 `npm run db:invariants` only.
#
# lib/db.ts reads DATABASE_URL and deliberately does not read DIRECT_URL. A
# route that quietly opened a session connection would work perfectly in
# development and exhaust the database in production — at traffic, not at
# deploy, which is the worst time to find out. Keep the pooler URL in the
# variable the app reads and the direct one out of its reach.
#
# Add ?sslmode=require to both. The pooler URL needs `?pgbouncer=true` only for
# tools that generate their own prepared statements; lib/db.ts already passes
# `prepare: false`, which is not optional — without it the pooler fails on the
# *second* request with "prepared statement already exists".
#
# Unset, the app still boots and the chat half still works: preview writes
# nothing by design, so a database is a production-only requirement. Production
# without it loses chat history, the purchases list and the one-card-per-customer
# binding — the money path, so the routes that need it answer 503 rather than
# minting a second card.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@<host>:6543/postgres?sslmode=require
# DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@<host>:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# ---- conversation history and quotas ---------------------------------------
# Redis, so the agent remembers what you said after the server restarts, and
# so the chat and faucet quotas are shared across instances.
#
# Required in production for the quotas: they fail closed, so production
# without these (or with Redis down) answers 503 on /api/chat and the faucet
# rather than letting unlimited turns through. Locally an in-memory counter
# stands in.
#
# Without it the app still works: history lives in the process, which is
# exactly right for one developer on one machine. On Vercel it is not — a cold
# start, a deploy or a scale-out mid-basket wipes it, and the agent answers
# with no idea what was asked while the page still shows every bubble.
#
# Vercel -> Storage -> Marketplace -> Upstash for Redis. Connecting the project
# injects these automatically, so you only need them here for local dev. Use
# the REST pair, not REDIS_URL or KV_URL: those are rediss:// strings for a TCP
# client, and one connection per lambda is the problem the REST API avoids.
# A database created directly at upstash.com names them UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL
# and UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN instead; both are read.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=

# ---- analytics (optional, public) ------------------------------------------
# Same names as the landing project, so one set of values can be copied onto
# the shopper Vercel project (apps/web) or a different set can be used.
# All three are browser ids. Leave them empty and the shopper loads no
# analytics scripts and records no events. A malformed value is ignored too.
#
# GA4 measurement id, Meta Pixel id, Microsoft Clarity project id.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARITY_PROJECT_ID=

# ---- optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Pin the ARS/USD rate instead of reading the live feed, so a demo quotes the
# same number every time. Unset means live.
FX_ARS_PER_USD=

# Optional. The agent model. Defaults to claude-sonnet-5. Haiku 4.5 runs (the
# loop switches to a fixed thinking budget for it), but in the one run measured
# here it stopped after the product search without building the cart.
# AGENT_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# Optional. Logs per-hop token usage to the server console.
# AGENT_USAGE=1

# ---- how hard the model thinks (optional) ----------------------------------
# low | medium | high. Defaults to low.
#
# This is the speed knob, not a quality knob in the usual direction. Adaptive
# thinking runs before *every* tool call, and a basket is up to twelve of them,
# so a second of extra deliberation per hop is twelve seconds the shopper
# spends watching "Buscando…". Each hop is a small, well-posed step with the
# tool schemas in front of it, which is not the kind of work that rewards it.
#
# Set medium to restore what shipped before. Move this first if baskets start
# coming back wrong rather than slow.
# AGENT_EFFORT=low
